window.docContents[63].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Characteristics___-Waves___-and-Nonlinear-Models":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems","header":"7.6.1. Characteristics, Waves, and Nonlinear Models","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Characteristics___-Waves___-and-Nonlinear-Models"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Symmetric-First-Order-Systems--Scope-of-the-energy-method":{"contents":"Mollifying the spatial operator first gives a bounded-operator evolution\nproblem. For coefficients continuous in time in spatial  norm,\nthe construction works for each  with  initial data\nand  forcing. Symmetric spatial  coefficients\nwith uniformly bounded derivative norms give an energy bound independent\nof .\n\nWith  initial data and  forcing, we can also pass to the\noriginal system. On a finite interval , with , assume the\nsymmetric matrix entries belong to spatial . Their values\nmust be continuous in time in spatial  norm on that interval,\nand their spatial derivative norms must be uniformly bounded there.\nUnder these hypotheses the construction gives an \nsolution of the original principal system,\nwith  time derivative and a continuous  representative\nattaining the initial data.\n\nThe proof bounds the mollified trajectories in , passes to a joint\nweak Bochner limit of states and time derivatives, and identifies the\nunmollified coefficient terms. Weak closedness preserves the time-dependent\nenergy bound. The integrated initial identity supplies the continuous\n trace. The principal system has no zeroth-order coefficient.\n\nThe same coefficient assumptions also allow merely  initial data and\nprescribed forcing  square-integrable on time-space. Almost every\nspatial slice of  defines an  class; constructing these classes\nas a Bochner forcing lets the Cauchy argument apply.\nThe resulting continuous  curve attains the initial datum. Its energy\nbound uses the sum of the product integrals of , without a factor\nfor the number of components.\n\nThe weak equation uses this same . Every joint product- state\nsatisfying that equation with the same initial boundary datum agrees with\nthe constructed state almost everywhere for the product measure.\nReconstructing a competitor's Bochner sections reduces this to uniqueness\nof the Bochner class, without assuming time regularity of the competitor.\nThe initial value is attained by the continuous  curve and enters the\nweak equation through its initial boundary term.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Symmetric First-Order Systems","header":"7.5.1.1. Scope of the energy method","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Symmetric-First-Order-Systems--Scope-of-the-energy-method"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Conservation-and-Incompressibility/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Conservation-and-Incompressibility--From-Conservation-Laws-to-Incompressible-Flow":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Conservation and Incompressibility","header":"8.2.1. From Conservation Laws to Incompressible Flow","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Conservation-and-Incompressibility/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Conservation-and-Incompressibility--From-Conservation-Laws-to-Incompressible-Flow"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--B-Splines-and-Schoenberg___Whitney-Collocation--Cox___de-Boor-B-splines":{"contents":"B-splines are the standard basis for certified piecewise-polynomial\napproximation. A collocation matrix with a checked positive determinant\ncertifies that a finite family is linearly independent. Tensor products of these one-dimensional\nfamilies provide profile representations on rectangles.\n\nA knot sequence is a nondecreasing mapbundled as an order homomorphism. Repeated knots are allowed, and a finite\nknot vector is represented by repeating its final knot with the desired\nendpoint multiplicity.\n\nMonotonicity of the knots is the only structure the recursion needs. The\nblending weights divide by the knot differences  and\n, which are nonnegative because the sequence is\nnondecreasing and may vanish when knots repeat.\n\nThe degree-zero B-spline with index  is the indicator of the half-open\nknot cell , and the recursiondefines the higher degrees, with the convention that a quotient with zero\ndenominator equals zero. That convention is Lean's totalized division, and it\nis exactly the standard treatment of repeated knots. A finite family selects\nthe indices  of a fixed degree.\n\nWith knots , , and , one step of the recursion\nproduces the familiar degree-one hat:\n\n\n\nOnly the two adjacent cells contribute. In a linear combination of such\nbasis functions, changing one coefficient changes the function only near\nthat coefficient's knot interval. The support theorem states this\nlocality. Whether specified sample values determine the coefficients\nuniquely is a different question, answered by the collocation theorem.\n\nThe recursion combines  and  with weights that are\nnonnegative on the supports  and  of\nthose two children, and both supports lie inside .\nInduction on the degree therefore propagates support and sign information\nfrom the degree-zero indicators, and the strict positivity statement is what\nthe Schoenberg–Whitney diagonal condition later relies on.\n\nFor every nondecreasing knot sequence, degree , and index ,so the B-spline vanishes for  and for . Moreover\nit is strictly positive on the open interior of its canonical support,including when interior knots are repeated.\n\nAll statements are proved by induction on the degree. At degree zero they\nare properties of the indicator of a half-open cell. In the recursion, a\nnonzero value requires one of the two child B-splines to be nonzero, and the\nchild supports  and  lie inside\n. On those supports both blending weights are\nnonnegative, giving nonnegativity. For strict positivity, away from a\nrepeated knot block one of the two children is positive by induction and its\nweight is positive; when the point coincides with an entire block of equal\nknots, the left-clamped identity  resolves the right-continuous\nconvention.\n\nNonnegativity and local support describe individual B-splines; linear\nindependence of a finite family is a statement about all of them at once,\nand the cleanest certificate for it is a matrix. Evaluating  functions at\n points gives a square matrix whose nonsingularity forces any vanishing\nlinear combination to have zero coefficients, so independence is transferred\nto the question of a nonzero determinant.\n\nFor a finite family of functions  and matching points\n, indexed by the same finite type, the collocation matrix isIf  is a field and , the family  is linearly\nindependent as -valued functions: a vanishing combination evaluated\nat the points gives a vanishing product with , and invertibility forces\nthe coefficients to vanish.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009B-Splines and Schoenberg–Whitney Collocation","header":"9.2.1.10.1. Cox–de Boor B-splines","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--B-Splines-and-Schoenberg___Whitney-Collocation--Cox___de-Boor-B-splines"}});